# The one DeepSeek Harness generation dshline currently targets.
#
# dshline tracks Harness aggressively and supports it narrowly: one adopted
# generation at a time, with obsolete assumptions deleted rather than carried
# behind a compatibility layer.
#
#   revision  the exact upstream commit at which Harness cut a coherent
#             release generation — the commit that release was published from,
#             not an arbitrary intermediate commit on master. The `Harness
#             target` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml checks this out, links it
#             with tools/link-harness.mjs, and blocks a merge on it.
#   version   the exact Harness version cut from that revision. Every dsh-*
#             dependency, devDependency, and peerDependency carries this
#             version literally — no carets, no `||` — so "one generation" is
#             a string comparison rather than a compatibility question.
#
# The two lines must describe ONE generation, and CI proves it: the target job
# reads the checked-out Harness workspace root's own version and fails if it
# disagrees with `version` below. Without that, a source lane and an npm lane
# could validate different generations and both pass.
#
# Adopting a release generation rather than a bare master commit is deliberate.
# It keeps the blocking development graph on something that has a coherent npm
# distribution, so `Harness target` and `Harness published` can describe the
# same thing. It is not a claim that dshline supports anything older: nothing
# watches upstream master, because an arbitrary master commit is not something
# this file can record. The non-blocking `Harness-Sync` workflow watches
# upstream's `dsh-v*` GitHub Releases instead and opens a pull request
# proposing the next generation, and dshline migrates onto it when we want it.
#
# Advancing this pointer IS the migration, and it is one commit: move dshline
# onto the new generation's native APIs, delete what the old one needed, run
# `node tools/harness-target.mjs --pin && pnpm install`, set the peer versions
# to match, and edit the two lines below. Never advance it to a revision
# dshline does not already work against — a green `Harness target` is the claim
# this file makes.

